[08:19] I built and saved both phone agents. Neither has been run on a customer: `public-test-set.json` is empty, so this kit gives these briefs no practice customers. As the skill says, I didn't run `tools/run_cases.py`. My only tests were the graph check and a read of each workflow against every rule in its brief. How the agents behave in a real call is still untested.
[08:19] 
[08:19] **Check results**
[08:19] - **Brief A:** 0 errors, 0 warnings.
[08:19] - **Brief B:** 0 errors, 1 warning. The warning says the `end_promise` step is never entered. That is expected: in B, every promise call reaches the `promise` ending through the precedence rules instead.
[08:19] 
[08:19] **How both agents work**
[08:19] - **Call flow:** the steps run in this order: date setup, confirming who answered, someone else on the line, security, loading the account, product setup, then getting the promise.
[08:19] - **Dates in code:** code works out the business days from `now`, using the memo's holiday list (including 26 October). It also computes the 12 and 7 business-day deadlines, the attempt-1 date, the "this/next weekday" dates and the callback window (today or the next business day, 10:00 to 19:00). I checked the deadline arithmetic by hand for several dates.
[08:19] - **Fixed endings:** all 16 close codes from the list are declared, each with a fixed thank-you and goodbye.
[08:19] - **Precedence rules:** these decide some endings from tool results, so the model cannot pick another one:
[08:19]   - a completed transfer always closes as transferred;
[08:19]   - a recorded promise closes as `promise`, unless a transfer follows it;
[08:19]   - no security attempts left closes as `not_verified/failed`.
[08:19] - **Separate transfer tools:** each transfer queue has its own tool, so the transfer reason comes from which tool succeeded.
[08:19] 
[08:19] **Brief B additions**
[08:19] - Promises are recorded and notes are added only in a final recording step, after one read-back of all products. That keeps notes out of transferred calls, as the change notice requires.
[08:19] - Each product has its own promise and note tool. Code-free routing steps choose the close reason: the first product, in account order, whose note is not `paid`. If every note is `paid`, the call closes as `paid`.
[08:19] 
[08:19] **Choices to know about**
[08:19] - **Account lookup failure:** the briefs give no close code for `get_account` failing. In that case the agent closes as `no_promise` with reason `service_error` and a different apology.
[08:19] - **Hardship in brief B:** the change notice doesn't say how hardship works with several products. I chose: no persuasion attempts on any remaining product, and every product without a promise is noted `hardship`.
[08:19] - **Hook on `verify_identity`:** this tool has a post-hook that tells the model what to say next. If the code sandbox fails on that call, the call would see a failure.
[08:19] 
[08:19] The generator is `out/gen_agents.py`. It writes the body and closing files for both briefs.
